// CRC-32, ISO-HDLC / ITU-T V.42 / IEEE 802.3, and CRC-32C, RFC 3720:
// https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3720
//
// A checksum, not a hash: it detects the kind of corruption a wire or a disk
// produces — a burst of flipped bits in one place — and detects nothing at all
// against someone who means to change the message. Use it for integrity, never
// for authentication.
//
// Both members are the same machine with a different polynomial. They are
// reflected, meaning the bits of each byte are processed least significant
// first, which in practice means shifting right and xoring the table entry into
// the low byte rather than the high one. CRC-32C uses a polynomial chosen so
// that it catches the kinds of multi-byte burst that the original does not, and
// is what iSCSI, ext4 and Btrfs write to disk.
import {
  "moonbitstack/mooncrypt/spec",
}

import {
  "moonbitstack/moonbase/base16",
} for "test"
